Rationale
- The extraction rule is chronological, not positional: each release block carries its own publish timestamp that is hours earlier than the surrounding traffic records, so physical line order inside a shard and the numeric suffix of a shard name are both unreliable proxies for time. Enumerating shards and comparing embedded timestamps is the only reading of "earliest" that survives a shuffled or regrouped log set; short-circuiting on the first match found would return whatever the filesystem or filename ordering surfaced first, which is nondeterministic across hosts. That nondeterminism is why the shard set was reasoned about from naming patterns rather than confirmed by a directory listing — an assumption carried forward as [A1] — and why a failed probe is treated as an unresolved enumeration question rather than as evidence that a shard is absent.
- 
- First-write-wins is applied per field, independently. A release record is an attestation of what was *first* published, so a later re-emission of an id or checksum is a second observation of the same publication; letting it overwrite would silently rewrite the historical fact the record exists to preserve. Conflating the fields into a single "current release" tuple was rejected for the same reason, as was treating the paired channel record as part of the identity tuple — it is context, not identity, and pairing must stay inside one release block rather than drifting across neighbouring records.
- 
- Request telemetry and the error/503 lines were classified as triage material for postmortem work, not as release identity; pursuing them would have spent effort on noise. Re-reading a shard already summarized by folded history was judged redundant rather than corroborating, and the redundancy was accepted deliberately: repeating a read is cheap and auditable, whereas reconstructing a value from memory of a prior extract is not.
